  "notes": "An intransitive verb describing the natural process of healing. Used for both physical wounds/illnesses and emotional or psychological pain. Somewhat literary in register — in everyday speech, {治|なお}る is more common for physical recovery.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {傷|きず}が{癒|い}える: a wound heals\n- {心|こころ}の{傷|きず}が{癒|い}える: emotional wounds heal\n- {病|やまい}が{癒|い}える: an illness is cured\n- {悲|かな}しみが{癒|い}える: sadness heals\n\nTRANSITIVITY:\nIntransitive. The transitive counterpart is {癒|いや}す (to heal, to soothe).\n\nREGISTER:\nSomewhat literary and formal. In everyday conversation, {治|なお}る (to get better, to recover) is preferred for physical healing. {癒|い}える is especially favored for emotional or spiritual healing.\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {治|なお}る: to get better, to recover (more common, especially for physical ailments)\n- {癒|いや}す: to heal, to soothe (transitive counterpart)\n- {回復|かいふく}する: to recover (formal, for health or systems)",
